00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:19.620
Building a cloud-native
application in our modern times

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:22.050
means embracing
one key concept--

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:23.880
embrace the open cloud.

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:27.000
This really means to write your
code, application, and systems

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.340
such that you can easily
move it between providers

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:31.950
without needing to worry
about heavy lifting.

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:33.390
Focusing on this
lets you utilize

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:35.130
the strengths of
each cloud provider,

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:37.129
while not being at the
mercy of vendor lock-in,

00:00:37.129 --> 00:00:39.420
not to mention the improved
resilience against provider

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:40.470
downtime.

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:43.590
Unfortunately, this is
much easier said than done.

00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:46.350
At a high level, most
clouds look the same.

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:49.260
But at a low level, things
are drastically different.

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:51.960
Each cloud has their own
provider-specific services,

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:55.440
unique APIs, specific
semantics, and in general

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.090
a different mental
model about how

00:00:57.090 --> 00:01:00.760
to architect, deploy, and manage
your cloud-native application.

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:03.040
That can make for a
lot of differences.

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:05.760
Kubernetes is one of the
tools that helps address this,

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:07.560
but it's not a
complete solution.

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:09.060
While Kubernetes
does a great job

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:11.100
at abstracting away
the underlying compute

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:13.350
infrastructure differences
between providers,

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:16.680
it doesn't extend into the
realm of database storage, log

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:19.110
aggregation, runtime
configuration, testing,

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:20.740
and so on, and so on.

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:23.220
So what do you do
about all that stuff?

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:25.720
Well, this is where Go
Cloud library comes in.

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:28.410
Go Cloud is an open-source
project written entirely

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:31.410
in Go lang, which provides
generic APIs for commonly

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:34.140
used services such
as blob storage,

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:38.010
runtime variables, MySQL
database access, and an HTTP

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:40.260
server configured with
tracing, request logging,

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:41.730
and health checks.

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:43.259
Using the abstraction
in Go Cloud

00:01:43.259 --> 00:01:45.300
lets you take your
application from being tightly

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.640
integrated into a
specific cloud vendor

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.622
to a more open and
generic platform.

00:01:49.622 --> 00:01:52.080
This removes very tight coupling
and gives your application

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:54.122
better portability
and flexibility.

00:01:54.122 --> 00:01:55.830
And that's one of the
ways you can really

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:57.206
embrace the open cloud.

00:01:57.206 --> 00:01:58.830
To help show off the
power of Go Cloud,

